1
00:00:04,060 --> 00:00:06,890
Gentlemen, I think I've come up with a fun way

2
00:00:06,890 --> 00:00:09,570
to get young people interested in science.

3
00:00:09,910 --> 00:00:12,160
Physics Mad-Libs.

4
00:00:14,030 --> 00:00:16,690
- Now, give me a number.  - Five.

5
00:00:18,340 --> 00:00:20,690
And an irrational constant.

6
00:00:20,740 --> 00:00:22,000
"E."

7
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:23,820
And a funny Greek letter.

8
00:00:23,820 --> 00:00:24,990
Gamma.

9
00:00:24,990 --> 00:00:26,540
I said funny.

10
00:00:27,880 --> 00:00:30,080
- Upsilon?  - Good one.

11
00:00:30,870 --> 00:00:32,540
And an electrical charge.

12
00:00:32,540 --> 00:00:33,690
Positive.

13
00:00:33,690 --> 00:00:35,000
Perfect.

14
00:00:36,310 --> 00:00:37,730
Okay. Get this.

15
00:00:38,220 --> 00:00:40,980
"Professor Jones told the symposium

16
00:00:40,980 --> 00:00:44,680
he had a new method for calculating the mass of a muon.

17
00:00:44,780 --> 00:00:50,920
Five times the limit of  'E' to the upsilon as..."

18
00:00:53,160 --> 00:00:54,240
Okay. No, no.

19
00:00:54,870 --> 00:00:56,180
I'll start over.

20
00:00:56,810 --> 00:00:57,930
"Professor..."

21
00:01:00,640 --> 00:01:02,330
I haven't seen him laugh that hard

22
00:01:02,330 --> 00:01:04,830
since the day Leonard made that multiplication error.

23
00:01:04,850 --> 00:01:07,550
Oh, Oh, Lord, that multiplication error!

24
00:01:07,820 --> 00:01:08,930
He thought he carried the one.

25
00:01:09,170 --> 00:01:10,140
But he didn't.

26
00:01:12,920 --> 00:01:13,820
It's not funny.

27
00:01:13,830 --> 00:01:14,840
That mistake got published.

28
00:01:14,840 --> 00:01:17,140
Stop! I'm going to wet myself!

29
00:01:18,100 --> 00:01:20,910
Hey, guys, guys, President Siebert is headed this way.

30
00:01:21,370 --> 00:01:23,310
- I wonder what he wants.  - Doesn't look happy,

31
00:01:23,310 --> 00:01:25,170
so I'm guessing he wants to talk to Sheldon.

32
00:01:25,170 --> 00:01:26,170
Dr. Cooper?

33
00:01:26,170 --> 00:01:26,620
Told ya.

34
00:01:27,040 --> 00:01:28,440
Oh, President Siebert,

35
00:01:28,490 --> 00:01:31,710
I assume you'd like to respond to one of the suggestions

36
00:01:31,710 --> 00:01:33,610
I put in the box by your office.

37
00:01:33,610 --> 00:01:36,810
No, and stop installing suggestion boxes everywhere.

38
00:01:37,830 --> 00:01:39,420
You don't like written suggestions.

39
00:01:39,420 --> 00:01:40,490
You don't like when I give them to you

40
00:01:40,490 --> 00:01:42,340
while we're urinating in the men's room.

41
00:01:42,820 --> 00:01:44,990
If I didn't know you any better, I'd say that you're one of those

42
00:01:44,990 --> 00:01:47,550
stubborn people who are not open to suggestions.

43
00:01:48,340 --> 00:01:50,470
Dr. Cooper, the physics department chair tells me

44
00:01:50,470 --> 00:01:52,260
you're refusing to take your vacation.

45
00:01:52,260 --> 00:01:53,540
I don't need a vacation.

46
00:01:53,540 --> 00:01:55,130
You're obligated to take one.

47
00:01:55,130 --> 00:01:57,140
And I'd also like you to know the most-often received

48
00:01:57,140 --> 00:02:00,370
suggestion in my suggestion box you installed without asking me

49
00:02:00,370 --> 00:02:02,610
is "Can Dr. Cooper take a vacation?"

50
00:02:04,370 --> 00:02:06,420
Okay, settled, then. I'll see y'all of you Monday,

51
00:02:06,710 --> 00:02:07,840
except for you.

52
00:02:07,840 --> 00:02:09,080
But if I don't come into work,

53
00:02:09,080 --> 00:02:10,480
what am I supposed to do with myself?

54
00:02:10,480 --> 00:02:12,880
Read, rest, travel.

55
00:02:12,880 --> 00:02:15,690
I hear Afghanistan is nice this time of year.

56
00:02:17,720 --> 00:02:19,060
Sarcasm?

57
00:02:19,490 --> 00:02:21,360
No. You should go.

58
00:02:48,900 --> 00:02:52,340
* That tonight's gonna be a good night *

59
00:02:52,340 --> 00:02:55,810
* And tonight's gonna be a good night *

60
00:02:55,810 --> 00:03:00,850
* And tonight's gonna be a good, good night *

61
00:03:00,850 --> 00:03:02,720
* Tonight's the night, uh, uh *

62
00:03:02,720 --> 00:03:04,220
* Let's live it up, uh *

63
00:03:04,220 --> 00:03:07,590
* I got my money, let's spend it up... *

64
00:03:07,590 --> 00:03:08,620
Good Lord!

65
00:03:09,290 --> 00:03:11,700
Would you stop that caterwauling!

66
00:03:14,360 --> 00:03:15,920
What the hell are you doing?!

67
00:03:15,920 --> 00:03:18,110
Bleeding from my ears.

68
00:03:18,770 --> 00:03:20,570
What are you doing hiding back there?

69
00:03:20,570 --> 00:03:22,240
I'm sneaking into work.

70
00:03:22,240 --> 00:03:24,440
Now, if the guard at the university asks

71
00:03:24,440 --> 00:03:25,540
what's under the blanket,

72
00:03:25,540 --> 00:03:27,560
you tell him it's some lobster traps.

73
00:03:28,700 --> 00:03:30,350
- Lobster traps?  - Yes.

74
00:03:30,350 --> 00:03:31,650
That's how Velma and Scooby

75
00:03:31,650 --> 00:03:33,750
smuggled Shaggy into the old lighthouse.

76
00:03:35,760 --> 00:03:37,720
What are you going to do when you get to the university?

77
00:03:37,720 --> 00:03:39,360
People are going to recognize you.

78
00:03:40,290 --> 00:03:42,590
Will they, Leonard?

79
00:03:47,600 --> 00:03:49,630
Fine. Just get back under your blanket,

80
00:03:49,630 --> 00:03:50,900
and I'll drive you there.

81
00:03:52,370 --> 00:03:53,800
And no more singing.

82
00:03:56,040 --> 00:03:57,230
Fine.

83
00:04:01,790 --> 00:04:03,580
I have GPS on my phone.

84
00:04:03,580 --> 00:04:05,010
I know you turned around.

85
00:04:08,170 --> 00:04:09,920
I'm so glad you talked Howard

86
00:04:09,920 --> 00:04:12,240
out of having your wedding invitations in Klingon.

87
00:04:14,690 --> 00:04:15,930
Turn it over.

88
00:04:18,390 --> 00:04:20,650
I'm hoping my relatives think it's Hebrew.

89
00:04:23,290 --> 00:04:24,700
This is really happening.

90
00:04:25,240 --> 00:04:26,740
I'm gonna be a maid of honor.

91
00:04:27,040 --> 00:04:29,740
I'm gonna wear a beautiful dress and walk down that aisle

92
00:04:29,740 --> 00:04:32,690
and, finally, I will have my special day.

93
00:04:33,940 --> 00:04:35,790
You mean my special day?

94
00:04:36,240 --> 00:04:39,260
They're gonna need an extra-large veil for somebody's head.

95
00:04:40,920 --> 00:04:42,850
If I ever actually ever get married.

96
00:04:42,850 --> 00:04:43,970
Why wouldn't you?

97
00:04:43,970 --> 00:04:45,290
My dad...

98
00:04:45,290 --> 00:04:47,120
'Cause I make a lot more money than Howie,

99
00:04:47,120 --> 00:04:49,460
he's putting a lot of pressure on me to get a prenup.

100
00:04:49,460 --> 00:04:50,790
Ouch.

101
00:04:50,790 --> 00:04:53,300
Yeah. Howie's gonna freak out.

102
00:04:53,300 --> 00:04:55,030
Parental pressure can be daunting.

103
00:04:55,030 --> 00:04:58,160
I remember the battle with my mother about shaving my legs.

104
00:04:59,700 --> 00:05:02,120
Last year, I finally gave in and let her do it.

105
00:05:04,840 --> 00:05:06,940
I just don't know how I'm gonna break it to him.

106
00:05:06,940 --> 00:05:09,110
You know, I'm a big believer in breaking bad news

107
00:05:09,110 --> 00:05:10,410
to a guy when you're in bed with him.

108
00:05:10,410 --> 00:05:11,970
That's how I told my high school boyfriend

109
00:05:11,970 --> 00:05:13,520
I slept with his brother.

110
00:05:14,920 --> 00:05:17,190
That's how I told his brother the same thing.

111
00:05:18,920 --> 00:05:21,590
I don't know, I don't want to manipulate him with sex.

112
00:05:21,590 --> 00:05:23,630
Oh, sweetie, that's what sex is for.

113
00:05:25,260 --> 00:05:27,970
You know, the connection between marriage and money is nothing new.

114
00:05:27,970 --> 00:05:31,030
In fact, the term "wed" referred to the money and livestock

115
00:05:31,030 --> 00:05:32,970
that the groom paid the bride's father.

116
00:05:32,970 --> 00:05:33,700
For example,

117
00:05:33,700 --> 00:05:34,980
you're adorable,

118
00:05:34,980 --> 00:05:36,610
intelligent and a good earner.

119
00:05:36,610 --> 00:05:38,510
I could conservatively see you going for

120
00:05:38,510 --> 00:05:40,510
at least two oxen and a goose.

121
00:05:42,330 --> 00:05:44,390
You would fetch a unicorn.

122
00:05:48,290 --> 00:05:51,320
Sheldon, there are a million great vacations you could take.

123
00:05:51,320 --> 00:05:52,570
What about Hawaii?

124
00:05:52,670 --> 00:05:57,660
Hawaii is a former leper colony on top of an active volcano

125
00:05:57,660 --> 00:06:00,460
where the disappointing ending to Lost was filmed.

126
00:06:03,220 --> 00:06:05,620
Mahalo for nothing, Hawaii.

127
00:06:06,240 --> 00:06:07,740
How about Florida?

128
00:06:07,740 --> 00:06:09,110
They got Cape Canaveral,

129
00:06:09,110 --> 00:06:11,040
they got Disney, they got my Aunt Ida

130
00:06:11,040 --> 00:06:13,510
and the world's largest collection of diabetic candy.

131
00:06:13,510 --> 00:06:15,280
Plus, if you get sweaty enough,

132
00:06:15,280 --> 00:06:18,230
her plastic-covered furniture is like a flume ride.

133
00:06:19,540 --> 00:06:22,600
My family took a trip to Florida when I was a child.

134
00:06:22,600 --> 00:06:25,980
A seagull stole a hot dog from me on the beach.

135
00:06:26,760 --> 00:06:27,860
I got the message.

136
00:06:29,360 --> 00:06:31,340
You know, if I had a week off, I'd go back

137
00:06:31,340 --> 00:06:33,720
to the Two Bunch Palms Resort and Spa in the desert.

138
00:06:33,720 --> 00:06:36,600
I tell you, an hour on the massage table with Trevor,

139
00:06:36,600 --> 00:06:39,740
and you'll feel like you were born without bones.

140
00:06:40,660 --> 00:06:44,340
I don't think I could ever let a guy give me a massage.

141
00:06:44,340 --> 00:06:45,890
Really? What was I doing to your neck last night

142
00:06:45,890 --> 00:06:47,630
while you were playing X-Box?

143
00:06:49,810 --> 00:06:51,980
It's like I'm living in a dictatorship.

144
00:06:51,980 --> 00:06:54,520
"You must take a vacation, you must have fun,

145
00:06:54,520 --> 00:06:56,080
you must enjoy life."

146
00:06:57,690 --> 00:07:00,520
I don't think you have a good handle on dictatorships.

147
00:07:01,430 --> 00:07:03,630
Sheldon, everybody takes vacations.

148
00:07:03,630 --> 00:07:06,300
One time they tried to make Richard Feynman take a vacation,

149
00:07:06,300 --> 00:07:08,630
but he chose instead to expand his mind

150
00:07:08,630 --> 00:07:09,870
and learn something new.

151
00:07:09,870 --> 00:07:12,270
He went to work in his friend's biology lab.

152
00:07:12,270 --> 00:07:15,170
Richard Feynman was a famous American physicist,

153
00:07:15,170 --> 00:07:16,710
part of the Manhattan Project.

154
00:07:17,600 --> 00:07:21,210
Everyone in the world of science knows who Richard Feynman was.

155
00:07:21,210 --> 00:07:22,680
Now you do, too.

156
00:07:25,440 --> 00:07:28,080
Oh! I have a brilliant idea.

157
00:07:28,080 --> 00:07:31,150
Amy's a biologist. I'll go work in her lab.

158
00:07:31,150 --> 00:07:33,720
Isn't that just Feynman's idea?

159
00:07:33,720 --> 00:07:35,000
Ten seconds ago, you never heard of him.

160
00:07:35,000 --> 00:07:36,500
Now you're an expert.

161
00:07:41,500 --> 00:07:43,350
- Hey, you. - Is that your laundry?

162
00:07:43,350 --> 00:07:44,930
You only have, like, six things in there.

163
00:07:44,930 --> 00:07:46,300
Yeah, I didn't have any quarters,

164
00:07:46,300 --> 00:07:49,000
so I've been sneaking stuff into other people's loads all day.

165
00:07:50,220 --> 00:07:51,370
Hey, if I tell you something,

166
00:07:51,370 --> 00:07:53,240
will you promise not to tell anybody?

167
00:07:53,240 --> 00:07:55,380
It doesn't matter what I say, you're gonna tell me anyway.

168
00:07:55,380 --> 00:07:58,850
What? That is not true.

169
00:08:00,380 --> 00:08:02,490
Bernadette wants a prenup.

170
00:08:03,150 --> 00:08:06,020
Wow. That's rough.

171
00:08:06,590 --> 00:08:08,890
So you're saying if I became a famous movie star,

172
00:08:08,890 --> 00:08:10,760
we got married, you wouldn't sign a prenup?

173
00:08:10,760 --> 00:08:12,430
Absolutely not.

174
00:08:12,500 --> 00:08:14,640
If I'm gonna be stuck at home with the kids

175
00:08:14,640 --> 00:08:16,020
while you're on location

176
00:08:16,020 --> 00:08:18,050
cheating on me with Ryan Gosling...

177
00:08:19,950 --> 00:08:21,900
then Leonard gots to get paid.

178
00:08:23,580 --> 00:08:26,210
So, you think about us getting married?

179
00:08:26,420 --> 00:08:28,010
I think about a lot of things.

180
00:08:28,010 --> 00:08:29,410
I think about us getting married,

181
00:08:29,410 --> 00:08:30,780
I think about us breaking up.

182
00:08:30,780 --> 00:08:32,410
Once in a while, I think about

183
00:08:32,420 --> 00:08:33,910
how I didn't leave a note on that Mercedes

184
00:08:33,910 --> 00:08:35,570
I dinged in the parking lot last month,

185
00:08:35,570 --> 00:08:38,010
but then I have a glass of wine and it passes.

186
00:08:38,220 --> 00:08:41,390
Joke all you want, but you think about it.

187
00:08:41,390 --> 00:08:42,420
Well, tell you one thing,

188
00:08:42,420 --> 00:08:45,630
if I ever do get married, no Klingon invitations.

189
00:08:46,660 --> 00:08:49,090
Good luck catching a man with that attitude.

190
00:08:51,320 --> 00:08:53,140
Boy, oh, boy!

191
00:08:53,140 --> 00:08:56,020
This vacation is off to a wonderful start.

192
00:08:56,620 --> 00:08:58,910
The smell of formaldehyde...

193
00:08:59,810 --> 00:09:02,240
the whir of the centrifuge,

194
00:09:02,250 --> 00:09:04,280
the distant chatter of lab animals

195
00:09:04,280 --> 00:09:06,140
being dispatched for dissection.

196
00:09:06,790 --> 00:09:10,220
Mm, I can already feel my cares just melting away.

197
00:09:10,820 --> 00:09:12,340
I'm excited to work with my boyfriend.

198
00:09:12,340 --> 00:09:13,900
It's gonna be romantic.

199
00:09:13,900 --> 00:09:15,520
Way to kill the mood.

200
00:09:17,410 --> 00:09:18,130
Come on, Sheldon.

201
00:09:18,130 --> 00:09:20,830
We can be like Marie Curie and her husband, Pierre,

202
00:09:20,830 --> 00:09:23,100
who spent their days working side by side,

203
00:09:23,100 --> 00:09:25,100
bathed in the glow of their love and the radium

204
00:09:25,100 --> 00:09:26,250
that ultimately killed her.

205
00:09:27,630 --> 00:09:28,700
Screw Beauty and the Beast;

206
00:09:28,700 --> 00:09:30,980
that's the love story Disney should tell.

207
00:09:32,100 --> 00:09:34,680
Okay, what do we start with?

208
00:09:34,680 --> 00:09:38,050
Maybe splicing some genes, clone a sheep,

209
00:09:38,050 --> 00:09:41,200
perhaps grow a human ear on a mouse's back?

210
00:09:41,200 --> 00:09:42,900
Ha-ha, I'm a freak!

211
00:09:45,560 --> 00:09:48,050
Oh, I'm gonna be doing some brain stem histology

212
00:09:48,050 --> 00:09:50,280
while you put yourself on the business end

213
00:09:50,280 --> 00:09:52,770
of a sponge and wash those beakers.

214
00:09:53,060 --> 00:09:54,270
Wash those bea...

215
00:09:54,270 --> 00:09:58,290
Oh, I get it, a little hazing for the new fella.

216
00:09:58,290 --> 00:10:00,230
Yeah, I'd better keep an eye out for, what,

217
00:10:00,230 --> 00:10:01,680
shoe polish on the microscope,

218
00:10:01,680 --> 00:10:04,430
or mad cow disease in my grilled cheese sandwich?

219
00:10:05,270 --> 00:10:07,140
No, I just need those beakers washed.

220
00:10:07,140 --> 00:10:08,580
Hippity-hop, quick like a bunny.

221
00:10:09,240 --> 00:10:12,550
Wha...? Excuse me, you have Dr. Sheldon Cooper in your lab.

222
00:10:12,550 --> 00:10:13,880
You're gonna make him do the dishes?

223
00:10:13,880 --> 00:10:15,420
That's like asking the Incredible Hulk

224
00:10:15,420 --> 00:10:17,040
to open a pickle jar.

225
00:10:19,670 --> 00:10:22,290
Sheldon, you've never worked in a lab like this before.

226
00:10:22,290 --> 00:10:24,890
You have no experience in the field of biology.

227
00:10:24,890 --> 00:10:27,330
I have plenty of experience in biology.

228
00:10:27,330 --> 00:10:29,760
I bought a Tamagotchi in 1998.

229
00:10:30,270 --> 00:10:31,370
And...

230
00:10:31,750 --> 00:10:32,840
it's still alive.

231
00:10:32,840 --> 00:10:34,270
Let's do this.

232
00:10:40,920 --> 00:10:43,090
- Where's Howard? - No "Hi, Raj"?

233
00:10:43,090 --> 00:10:44,690
No "How are you, Raj?"

234
00:10:44,690 --> 00:10:47,080
Just straight to "Where's the other white guy?"

235
00:10:48,300 --> 00:10:49,430
I'm sorry.

236
00:10:49,870 --> 00:10:52,360
So, listen, I heard something about him.

237
00:10:52,360 --> 00:10:54,100
Can you keep it between us?

238
00:10:54,100 --> 00:10:55,500
Ooh, gossip.

239
00:10:55,500 --> 00:10:57,870
When I first got here, I thought you Americans

240
00:10:57,870 --> 00:10:59,570
really gossiped around the water cooler.

241
00:10:59,580 --> 00:11:01,540
So I hung out there for, like, a month,

242
00:11:01,540 --> 00:11:03,700
but the only gossip I ever heard was about some creepy guy

243
00:11:03,700 --> 00:11:05,420
hanging out by the water cooler.

244
00:11:07,820 --> 00:11:09,990
Bernadette wants to get a prenup.

245
00:11:09,990 --> 00:11:12,710
Oh, that's a shame-- he's gonna be devastated.

246
00:11:12,710 --> 00:11:14,820
I never know what to do in these situations.

247
00:11:14,820 --> 00:11:16,220
Should I give him a heads-up?

248
00:11:16,230 --> 00:11:18,760
Hmm. I'm gonna give you the same advice

249
00:11:18,760 --> 00:11:21,900
I yell at the TV when the Bachelor's handing out roses.

250
00:11:23,770 --> 00:11:25,580
Follow your heart.

251
00:11:27,350 --> 00:11:28,830
Check it out.

252
00:11:28,830 --> 00:11:31,570
Look at the size of that Rice Krispie Treat!

253
00:11:31,570 --> 00:11:33,580
Same price.

254
00:11:34,190 --> 00:11:35,980
Hey, Howard, I need to tell you something.

255
00:11:35,980 --> 00:11:37,810
I know, it's not on my wedding diet.

256
00:11:37,810 --> 00:11:38,540
I don't care.

257
00:11:41,530 --> 00:11:43,550
Uh, l-listen, I...

258
00:11:43,550 --> 00:11:46,930
heard that Bernadette's thinking about asking you for a prenup.

259
00:11:48,060 --> 00:11:49,460
A prenup?

260
00:11:52,640 --> 00:11:54,000
What are you gonna do?

261
00:11:54,670 --> 00:11:57,670
I... don't know.

262
00:11:57,670 --> 00:11:59,200
Follow your heart.

263
00:12:05,790 --> 00:12:09,230
You know what, it's... not a big deal.

264
00:12:09,230 --> 00:12:11,510
She makes more money than me.

265
00:12:11,510 --> 00:12:15,140
She wants to protect her financial interests.

266
00:12:15,390 --> 00:12:16,950
It's completely reasonable.

267
00:12:16,950 --> 00:12:18,390
Good. That's a healthy attitude.

268
00:12:18,390 --> 00:12:20,390
Yeah, actually, it's good for both of us.

269
00:12:20,390 --> 00:12:21,890
I have assets to protect, too.

270
00:12:21,890 --> 00:12:23,250
Like what?

271
00:12:24,190 --> 00:12:26,240
I've got some rare comic books.

272
00:12:26,270 --> 00:12:28,780
The Vespa's almost paid off.

273
00:12:29,730 --> 00:12:32,800
And Ma and I have a primo double cemetery plot at Mt. Sinai

274
00:12:32,800 --> 00:12:34,970
right near the guy who played Mr. Roper on Three's Company.

275
00:12:36,260 --> 00:12:38,060
Mr. Roper's dead?!

276
00:12:40,420 --> 00:12:42,450
You can't just spring that on a guy!

277
00:12:45,570 --> 00:12:46,750
Here you go.

278
00:12:46,750 --> 00:12:48,620
This is now the only lab

279
00:12:48,620 --> 00:12:51,320
with glassware washed by a man with two doctorates

280
00:12:51,320 --> 00:12:53,970
and a restraining order signed by Carl Sagan.

281
00:12:55,830 --> 00:12:57,600
Soap spots. Wash 'em again.

282
00:12:57,600 --> 00:12:59,490
Y... You're being ridiculous!

283
00:12:59,500 --> 00:13:01,560
Those are perfectly clean.

284
00:13:02,150 --> 00:13:04,500
Sheldon, this beaker used to contain cerebral spinal fluid

285
00:13:04,500 --> 00:13:06,740
from an elephant that died of syphilis.

286
00:13:06,740 --> 00:13:08,370
If it's, in fact, perfectly clean,

287
00:13:08,410 --> 00:13:09,720
drink from it.

288
00:13:16,760 --> 00:13:18,460
Biologists are mean.

289
00:13:21,090 --> 00:13:24,280
All right, perhaps this task will be a little bit more up your alley.

290
00:13:24,280 --> 00:13:27,160
I need you to count the bacteria spores on these petri dishes.

291
00:13:27,160 --> 00:13:29,320
There was something wrong with that detergent.

292
00:13:29,330 --> 00:13:31,080
That was way too bubbly.

293
00:13:31,970 --> 00:13:33,290
- I'm sure it was. - I intend to write

294
00:13:33,300 --> 00:13:35,590
that soap company a strongly worded letter.

295
00:13:36,170 --> 00:13:38,600
Yeah, good for you. Now, start counting.

296
00:13:39,640 --> 00:13:41,640
You know what this place needs?

297
00:13:41,640 --> 00:13:43,250
A suggestion box.

298
00:13:45,690 --> 00:13:47,780
366...

299
00:13:47,780 --> 00:13:49,740
367...

300
00:13:49,750 --> 00:13:51,180
How's it going?

301
00:13:51,460 --> 00:13:53,520
How's counting going?

302
00:13:53,520 --> 00:13:54,970
When I was in kindergarten,

303
00:13:54,970 --> 00:13:58,940
I recited pi to a thousand places for the school talent show.

304
00:13:58,940 --> 00:14:02,270
- I think I got this.  - Great.

305
00:14:06,960 --> 00:14:08,730
Aw, nuts!

306
00:14:11,700 --> 00:14:13,010
One...

307
00:14:15,230 --> 00:14:17,020
This is preposterous!

308
00:14:17,020 --> 00:14:18,640
I think you're giving me these tasks

309
00:14:18,640 --> 00:14:21,280
because you're afraid if you give me anything meaningful to do,

310
00:14:21,280 --> 00:14:22,610
I'll show you up.

311
00:14:23,380 --> 00:14:25,410
Really? Is that what you think?

312
00:14:25,410 --> 00:14:26,950
Yes, that's what I think.

313
00:14:26,950 --> 00:14:29,230
And I'm super smart, so it's probably true.

314
00:14:31,190 --> 00:14:34,210
Hey, I've been training in the field of neurobiology for 12 years.

315
00:14:34,210 --> 00:14:35,460
You've been here for three hours,

316
00:14:35,460 --> 00:14:37,550
and you've spent one of them in the bathroom.

317
00:14:37,960 --> 00:14:40,160
I'm sorry! It takes me a while

318
00:14:40,160 --> 00:14:42,560
to get things going on an unfamiliar toilet.

319
00:14:43,740 --> 00:14:45,600
Sheldon, I've given you the simplest things to do,

320
00:14:45,600 --> 00:14:47,770
and you haven't done one of them right.

321
00:14:47,770 --> 00:14:49,640
Maybe that's because I'm not being challenged.

322
00:14:49,640 --> 00:14:52,340
It's the same reason Einstein failed math.

323
00:14:52,340 --> 00:14:54,300
Maybe the math was too bubbly for him.

324
00:14:54,440 --> 00:14:57,550
You think you're doing science by cutting up that brain?

325
00:14:57,550 --> 00:14:59,530
They could do the same thing at any Quiznos.

326
00:14:59,530 --> 00:15:02,220
And they'd offer to toast it for me, too.

327
00:15:03,870 --> 00:15:05,140
Okay, smart guy.

328
00:15:05,140 --> 00:15:06,990
I'm about to remove the locus coeruleus,

329
00:15:06,990 --> 00:15:09,130
which is incredibly delicate work.

330
00:15:10,070 --> 00:15:11,020
Have at it.

331
00:15:13,010 --> 00:15:14,200
All right.

332
00:15:15,390 --> 00:15:18,090
I'm no stranger to a little gray matter.

333
00:15:21,270 --> 00:15:23,340
Locus coeruleus.

334
00:15:26,080 --> 00:15:28,620
Locus coeruleus.

335
00:15:28,910 --> 00:15:32,020
You're getting warmer-- it is, indeed, in the brain.

336
00:15:34,020 --> 00:15:35,120
Hope your hands are steady.

337
00:15:35,120 --> 00:15:37,150
It's the width of a single hair.

338
00:15:37,150 --> 00:15:38,470
But this is just biology,

339
00:15:38,470 --> 00:15:40,540
so I'm sure it's no problem for a genius like you.

340
00:15:40,540 --> 00:15:43,270
It's not. I'll have you know, in the field of physics,

341
00:15:43,270 --> 00:15:44,960
we work with particles so small,

342
00:15:44,960 --> 00:15:47,600
they make fat jokes about the locus coeruleus.

343
00:15:49,330 --> 00:15:52,100
I.e., when your locus coeruleus sits around the house,

344
00:15:52,100 --> 00:15:54,160
it sits around the house.

345
00:15:58,570 --> 00:16:01,590
Oh... are we nervous, Dr. Cooper?

346
00:16:01,590 --> 00:16:02,870
No.

347
00:16:02,870 --> 00:16:04,510
What you see

348
00:16:04,510 --> 00:16:07,930
is a man trembling with confidence.

349
00:16:11,950 --> 00:16:14,560
Does a locus coeruleus normally bleed that much?

350
00:16:14,560 --> 00:16:17,380
No. But your thumb does.

351
00:16:19,730 --> 00:16:21,600
Oh, dear.

352
00:16:25,270 --> 00:16:27,480
Yeah, you're a biologist.

353
00:16:32,510 --> 00:16:33,840
What are you doing here?

354
00:16:34,320 --> 00:16:36,030
I'm on vacation.

355
00:16:36,220 --> 00:16:37,800
Social convention dictates

356
00:16:37,800 --> 00:16:41,030
that I let my hair down at a local watering hole.

357
00:16:43,710 --> 00:16:45,820
Social convention is stupid.

358
00:16:47,860 --> 00:16:49,410
What happened to your thumb?

359
00:16:49,770 --> 00:16:51,950
I have ten fingers and ten toes.

360
00:16:51,950 --> 00:16:53,860
If I tell you a story about each one of them,

361
00:16:53,860 --> 00:16:55,930
we'll be here all day-- let's just move on.

362
00:16:57,170 --> 00:16:58,090
What can I get you?

363
00:16:58,090 --> 00:17:00,670
Ah, seeing as I'm on vacation,

364
00:17:00,670 --> 00:17:03,100
uh, Pina Colada seems appropriate.

365
00:17:03,110 --> 00:17:05,840
Extra pineapple slices, extra whipped cream,

366
00:17:05,840 --> 00:17:08,080
extra cherries, extra umbrellas,

367
00:17:08,080 --> 00:17:10,180
and, uh, hold the rum.

368
00:17:11,550 --> 00:17:13,310
Don't let me have too many of those.

369
00:17:14,620 --> 00:17:16,550
Hey, what are you guys doing here?

370
00:17:16,970 --> 00:17:19,710
We're grown men; we drink at bars.

371
00:17:19,920 --> 00:17:22,050
No and no.

372
00:17:24,310 --> 00:17:26,260
Everything okay with you and Bernadette?

373
00:17:26,260 --> 00:17:28,260
Oh, yeah, sure.

374
00:17:28,260 --> 00:17:29,930
You and Amy? Good?

375
00:17:29,930 --> 00:17:31,970
Oh, better than good.

376
00:17:33,410 --> 00:17:36,900
You know those girls text me every detail of their lives as it happens.

377
00:17:36,910 --> 00:17:38,630
I'm not signing a prenup.

378
00:17:39,440 --> 00:17:41,490
All right, Howard Wolowitz, listen up.

379
00:17:42,120 --> 00:17:43,680
You sign anything she puts in front of you,

380
00:17:43,680 --> 00:17:45,510
because you are the luckiest man alive.

381
00:17:45,510 --> 00:17:46,410
If you let her go,

382
00:17:46,410 --> 00:17:48,020
there is no way you can find anyone else--

383
00:17:48,020 --> 00:17:49,180
speaking on behalf of all women,

384
00:17:49,180 --> 00:17:51,150
it is not gonna happen; we had a meeting.

385
00:17:53,650 --> 00:17:55,540
And you, a grown man

386
00:17:55,540 --> 00:17:58,950
- fainting at the sight of a little blood. - Excuse me,

387
00:17:58,950 --> 00:18:01,450
this is a fairly substantial wound.

388
00:18:01,450 --> 00:18:02,450
You...

389
00:18:08,180 --> 00:18:11,910
Amy? Amy? Amy?

390
00:18:14,260 --> 00:18:15,580
What do you want?

391
00:18:15,910 --> 00:18:20,840
I was kind of hoping I could continue vacationing in your laboratory.

392
00:18:20,950 --> 00:18:23,560
After all, I did book the whole week.

393
00:18:25,640 --> 00:18:28,360
Do you honestly think you can just waltz back in here

394
00:18:28,360 --> 00:18:30,220
after the way you behaved yesterday?

395
00:18:30,270 --> 00:18:32,530
I was not myself.

396
00:18:32,530 --> 00:18:35,390
I had lost a lot of thumb blood.

397
00:18:37,730 --> 00:18:39,030
That's not an apology.

398
00:18:39,030 --> 00:18:41,030
That is your opinion.

399
00:18:41,690 --> 00:18:43,910
I want a real apology.

400
00:18:45,240 --> 00:18:49,430
- I'm sorry that you weren't able to... - No.

401
00:18:50,750 --> 00:18:52,000
...that my genius...

402
00:18:52,000 --> 00:18:53,370
No.

403
00:18:53,370 --> 00:18:54,930
...that the soap was...

404
00:18:54,930 --> 00:18:56,380
Sheldon.

405
00:18:59,050 --> 00:19:00,110
Fine.

406
00:19:03,700 --> 00:19:05,110
Sorry.

407
00:19:06,060 --> 00:19:07,680
You're forgiven.

408
00:19:08,310 --> 00:19:11,000
Now, if you want to stay, get started on those beakers.

409
00:19:11,000 --> 00:19:12,870
They're still dirty from yesterday.

410
00:19:17,030 --> 00:19:19,150
Next year I'm going to EPCOT.

411
00:19:25,550 --> 00:19:27,090
Are you mad at me?

412
00:19:27,550 --> 00:19:29,550
No. I'm not mad at you.

413
00:19:29,550 --> 00:19:30,130
I just...

414
00:19:30,690 --> 00:19:32,350
wish you would have come to me,

415
00:19:32,360 --> 00:19:35,290
so I didn't have to hear it through the nerd-vine.

416
00:19:37,890 --> 00:19:39,260
So, what are we gonna do?

417
00:19:40,830 --> 00:19:42,820
You really want me to sign a prenup?

418
00:19:42,820 --> 00:19:44,410
I don't know.

419
00:19:44,410 --> 00:19:46,870
My dad's pretty insistent on it, though.

420
00:19:46,870 --> 00:19:50,110
Why don't I talk to your dad, man-to-man?

421
00:19:50,110 --> 00:19:52,680
Really? Oh, that'd be so great!

422
00:19:52,680 --> 00:19:53,450
Done.

423
00:19:55,080 --> 00:19:58,010
I should probably give you a heads-up about a couple of things.

424
00:19:59,450 --> 00:20:01,450
Even though he's retired from the police force,

425
00:20:01,450 --> 00:20:02,890
he still carries his gun.

426
00:20:04,420 --> 00:20:05,610
But don't worry, he won't shoot it.

427
00:20:05,610 --> 00:20:07,840
It's more of a fashion statement.

428
00:20:09,460 --> 00:20:10,500
Okay.

429
00:20:10,890 --> 00:20:13,760
And just to be safe, when you talk to him,

430
00:20:13,760 --> 00:20:16,130
don't bring up Jimmy Carter, gardeners,

431
00:20:16,130 --> 00:20:18,110
foreign people, homosexuals, Sean Penn,

432
00:20:18,110 --> 00:20:20,540
Vatican II, gun control, organic food,

433
00:20:20,540 --> 00:20:23,690
the designated hitter rule, recycling or the fact that you're Jewish.

434
00:20:27,440 --> 00:20:29,060
Got it, got it.

435
00:20:29,060 --> 00:20:30,960
Will you e-mail me that list?

436
00:20:32,810 --> 00:20:34,460
So the thing to watch for,

437
00:20:34,460 --> 00:20:36,720
if he's shouting at you, you're okay,

438
00:20:36,720 --> 00:20:38,720
but if he starts to get real quiet,

439
00:20:38,720 --> 00:20:41,730
leave as quickly as you can without making eye contact.

440
00:20:43,360 --> 00:20:46,190
Not in a straight line-- throw some zigs and zigs in there.

441
00:20:49,510 --> 00:20:51,510
You know, this isn't that pressing.

442
00:20:51,510 --> 00:20:53,770
Why don't I talk to him about it in May.

443
00:20:53,970 --> 00:20:56,960
In May you're gonna be on the International Space Station.

444
00:20:57,980 --> 00:20:59,760
They got a phone.

